A||||2||Return of Fiero||prep work for an engine harness???||||5||
Z||000000||Return of Fiero||10-26-2001||03:54 PM||||is there anything i should do to the engine harness before i put it in my 84 2M4? any process that might keep the harness from becoming burnt toast? time to start the lawn ornament..<BR>Myke||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tigger||10-26-2001||08:46 PM||||Spray it with simple green, scrub the conduit with a scrub brush, carefully rinse avoiding connectors, pat dry and hang up overnight.  Replace any unraveling black tape, fix any frays.  Lastly give it a dash of Formula 2001 to give the conduit some shine.  Clean any bad connectors and spray with some electrical grease to displace any moisture.<P>When installing, try to use all of the original clips etc. and avoid hot surfaces.<P>Probably would be a good idea to get some heat wrap tape for the bundle of wires that come out from the firewall to the engine, and the wires that go to the starter.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-27-2001||01:22 PM||||If the plastic conduit is anything like most cars.... replace it. you can get that stuff at many parts stores and it's not very expensive.<P>Don't use electrical tape to replace the black factory wrap. They aren't the same. the factory stuff is non adhesive and thicker than most electrical tape. The only source I can think of that I know for sure has the correct tape is <A HREF="http://www.eastwoodco.com" TARGET=_blank>Eastwood</A> but you can try the regular parts stores as well.<P>There's a tape that you can get thru, I think Summit, and other performance shops. maybe some parts stores. It's a metalic tape made specifically to be heat reflective to protect the wiring. You'll want that for anything that goes around the front of the motor.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
